lative clause with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify who/which/that and explain why it matters.","I can practise ending independently on a new prompt.","I can notice precise detail needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Identifying relative clauses (who, which, that) add a precise detail about a noun so readers know which person or thing is meant; endings should still answer the story’s goal.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Identifying relative clauses (who, which, that) add a precise detail about a noun so readers know which person or thing","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about relative clause with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"The student who lost the label checked the board again.\" The clause who lost the label identifies which student. Spelling check: hurry → hurried (y to i); stop → stopped (doubling). Ending: she finds the label and pins it before visitors arrive.\n\nTRY FIRST\nAdd a who/which/that clause to \"The envelope sat on the shelf\" and write one past-tense verb that needs y-to-i or doubling.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: \"The envelope that Nia found sat on the shelf.\" Verbs: tried, dropped, planned — formed with the taught patterns.","explain":"Identifying relative clauses (who, which, that) add a precise detail about a noun so readers know which person or thing is meant; endings should still answer the story’s goal.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against 